WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.910
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.910
What is an acoustic filter?

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:13.599
Well, it is an arbitrarily shaped object that
takes a sound as an input, and outputs a different

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:14.679
sound.

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:19.040
These filters have some really amazing applications
that you'll hear about in a minute.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.210
In this work, a novel technique is proposed
to automatically design such filters.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.390
It works by building an arbitrarily shaped
object as a set of connected tiny resonators,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:36.100
and chooses appropriate sizes and setups for
each of these elements to satisfy a prescribed

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.219
set of acoustic properties.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:43.320
Instead of resorting to a lengthy and flimsy
trial and error phase, we can use physics

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.750
to simulate what would happen if we were to
use a given arrangement in reality.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.739
Again, one of those works that have a deep
connection to the real world around us.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:55.739
Absolutely amazing.

00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:01.410
The goal can be to eliminate the peaks of
the sound of a car horn or an airplane engine,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.159
and we can achieve this objective by means
of optimization.

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:09.280
The proposed applications we can divide into
three main categories:

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:15.720
The first is identifying and filtering noise
attenuation components for a prescribed application,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.770
to which we can also refer to as muffler design.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:24.650
In simpler words, we are interested in filtering
or muffling the peaks of a known signal.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:29.159
Designing such objects is typically up to
trial and error and in this case, it is even

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:34.890
harder because we're interested in a wider
variety of shape choices other than exhaust

00:01:34.890 --> 00:02:03.490
pipes and tubes that are typically used in
the industry.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:33.739
Second, designing musical instruments is hard,
and unless we design them around achieving

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:38.760
a given acoustic response, we'll likely end
up with inharmonious gibberish.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:46.150
And this method also supports designing musical
instruments with, hmm, well, non-conventional

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:56.300
shapes.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:09.720
Well, this is as non-conventional as it gets
I'm afraid.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.659
And also, that is about the most harmonious
sound I've heard coming out of the rear end

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:16.020
of a hippo.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.260
And third, this work opens up the possibility
of making hollow objects that are easy to

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.410
identify by means of acoustic tagging.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:04:21.100
Check out this awesome example that involves
smacking these 3d printed piggies.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:26.139
If you feel like improving your kung fu in
math, there are tons of goodies such as transmission

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:29.500
matrices, the Helmholtz equation, oh my!

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.080
The paper and the talk slides are amazingly
well written, and yes, you should definitely

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.370
have a look at them.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.280
Let us know in the comments section if you
have some ideas for possible applications

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.600
beyond these ones, we love to read your take
on these works.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.370
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

